SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','adverbs-position','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"adverbs-position","title":"Adverbs of frequency, manner and position","level":"A2","order":24,"summary":"Describe how often and how an action happens, while placing the adverb where its meaning is clear.","guidance":"Read the form and meaning together. Study the contrasts, then explain a worked example in your own words before starting a fresh practice session.","learningGoals":["Frequency before the main verb","After be and after the first auxiliary","Manner and adjective forms","Keep the object beside its verb"],"rules":[{"title":"Frequency before the main verb","explanation":"Frequency adverbs such as usually, often and never normally go before an ordinary lexical verb. Their position may shift for focus, but neutral order gives a reliable starting point. Never is already negative in meaning.","pattern":"She **usually walks**. / They **never complain**.","commonMistakes":["Do not add not merely because never is negative: they never complain is already negative in meaning."],"examples":["Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the visitor guide. → **She usually prepares the visitor guide.**","Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the old portrait. → **She usually restores the old portrait.**"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"After be and after the first auxiliary","explanation":"With main-verb be, a frequency adverb usually follows be. With a multi-part verb phrase, it often follows the first auxiliary. Always can appear in other positions for emphasis; these are neutral-order exercises.","pattern":"She is **usually** early. / They have **often** visited.","commonMistakes":["She usually is early can be emphatic, so a neutral-order instruction is needed when comparing positions."],"examples":["Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to prepare the visitor guide.”","Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Manner and adjective forms","explanation":"A manner adverb describes how an action is done. Many are formed with -ly, but not all: fast and hard are common adverbs without -ly. An adjective usually describes a noun or follows a linking verb.","pattern":"They worked **carefully**. / The work was **careful**.","commonMistakes":["Hard means with effort; hardly means almost not. They are not ordinary form variants with the same meaning."],"examples":["Describe the action’s manner: “They prepare the visitor guide **carefully**.”","Describe the action’s manner: “They restore the old portrait **carefully**.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Keep the object beside its verb","explanation":"A short manner adverb often goes after the object, or before the main verb. Do not normally put it between a transitive verb and its direct object. Position also changes scope: only can focus on different parts of a sentence.","pattern":"She read **the letter carefully**.","commonMistakes":["She read carefully the letter is awkward neutral order; put carefully after the object or before read."],"examples":["Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the visitor guide. → **They prepared the visitor guide carefully.**","Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the old portrait. → **They restored the old portrait carefully.**"],"skill":"application"}],"tips":[],"source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"chapter":{"introduction":["Describe how often and how an action happens, while placing the adverb where its meaning is clear.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Frequency before the main verb","explanation":"Frequency adverbs such as usually, often and never normally go before an ordinary lexical verb. Their position may shift for focus, but neutral order gives a reliable starting point. Never is already negative in meaning.","pattern":"She **usually walks**. / They **never complain**.","commonMistakes":["Do not add not merely because never is negative: they never complain is already negative in meaning."],"examples":["Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the visitor guide. → **She usually prepares the visitor guide.**","Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the old portrait. → **She usually restores the old portrait.**"],"skill":"form","id":"adverbs-position-foundation-1","domain":"modifiers","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"After be and after the first auxiliary","explanation":"With main-verb be, a frequency adverb usually follows be. With a multi-part verb phrase, it often follows the first auxiliary. Always can appear in other positions for emphasis; these are neutral-order exercises.","pattern":"She is **usually** early. / They have **often** visited.","commonMistakes":["She usually is early can be emphatic, so a neutral-order instruction is needed when comparing positions."],"examples":["Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to prepare the visitor guide.”","Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning","id":"adverbs-position-foundation-2","domain":"modifiers","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Manner and adjective forms","explanation":"A manner adverb describes how an action is done. Many are formed with -ly, but not all: fast and hard are common adverbs without -ly. An adjective usually describes a noun or follows a linking verb.","pattern":"They worked **carefully**. / The work was **careful**.","commonMistakes":["Hard means with effort; hardly means almost not. They are not ordinary form variants with the same meaning."],"examples":["Describe the action’s manner: “They prepare the visitor guide **carefully**.”","Describe the action’s manner: “They restore the old portrait **carefully**.”"],"skill":"contrast","id":"adverbs-position-foundation-3","domain":"modifiers","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Keep the object beside its verb","explanation":"A short manner adverb often goes after the object, or before the main verb. Do not normally put it between a transitive verb and its direct object. Position also changes scope: only can focus on different parts of a sentence.","pattern":"She read **the letter carefully**.","commonMistakes":["She read carefully the letter is awkward neutral order; put carefully after the object or before read."],"examples":["Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the visitor guide. → **They prepared the visitor guide carefully.**","Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the old portrait. → **They restored the old portrait carefully.**"],"skill":"application","id":"adverbs-position-foundation-4","domain":"modifiers","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"domain":"negation","id":"near-negatives-polarity","title":"Hardly, scarcely, seldom and negative-polarity words","explanation":"Hardly, scarcely, rarely and seldom have negative or near-negative meaning without not. They often combine with any or ever and usually take positive question tags. Some expressions prefer negative, interrogative or conditional environments. Ever, any and at all are useful examples, although each has other licensed uses.","pattern":"She **hardly ever** complains.","examples":["There was **hardly any** traffic.","He **seldom visits**, does he?"],"commonMistakes":["Hardly means almost not; hard describes effort or intensity. They are not interchangeable adjective/adverb forms."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-negation","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"modifiers","id":"adverb-scope-and-focus","title":"Only, even, also and the reach of an adverb","explanation":"Focusing adverbs select a part of the message for contrast. Moving only can change whether the restriction concerns a person, action, object or time; stress also helps. Even marks something as unexpected on a relevant scale, while also adds an alternative. Put the adverb where its intended scope is easiest to recover.","pattern":"**Only Maya** borrowed it. / Maya borrowed **only it**.","examples":["I **only borrowed** the book; I did not buy it.","**Even the expert** was surprised."],"commonMistakes":["An adverb’s nearest word is not always its only possible focus in speech. In careful writing, choose placement that reduces ambiguity."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"modifiers","id":"sentence-adverbs-and-flat-adverbs","title":"Sentence adverbs, viewpoint adverbs and flat forms","explanation":"Some adverbs comment on a whole proposition or on the act of speaking: probably, fortunately, frankly. Others describe manner, degree or a particular viewpoint. Several have the same form as an adjective, such as fast and hard; a related -ly form may change meaning. Identify the job of the word in the sentence.","pattern":"**Frankly**, I disagree. / She answered **frankly**.","examples":["He arrived **late**, but I haven’t seen him **lately**.","The team played **well**, although the pitch was **bad**."],"commonMistakes":["Good is normally an adjective; well commonly describes how an action is done, but well can also be an adjective meaning healthy."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"}],"recap":["She **usually walks**. / They **never complain**.","She is **usually** early. / They have **often** visited.","They worked **carefully**. / The work was **careful**.","She read **the letter carefully**."],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["comparatives-and-superlatives"],"readingReferenceIds":["bc-adverbials","cambridge-negation","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"]}'::jsonb FROM grammar.content_catalog_topics WHERE version='core-2026-09-v3' AND slug='adverbs-position'
